13. Concrete scenarios the AI must handle

Create deterministic tests for the following scenarios, using real game systems wherever possible.

Scenario A: Dangerous Launch Point

The CPU has high Launch Point while the opponent has low Launch Point.

There is substantial time remaining.

Expected: Depending on the actual positions and threats, the CPU becomes more protective, considers disengagement, seeks safer territory, and may wait or Shield to recover.

It should not automatically rush into another dangerous exchange.

Scenario B: Opponent vulnerable to elimination

The opponent has high Launch Point near a dangerous boundary.

The CPU has suitable attacks available.

Expected: The CPU considers positioning, launch direction, and finisher opportunities rather than simply using whichever attack deals the most immediate damage.

Scenario C: Protecting a lead

The CPU is ahead 2–1 with little time remaining.

Expected: It prioritizes sensible survival and avoids unnecessary risky aggression.

It may still counterattack when safe.

Scenario D: Desperate comeback

The CPU is losing 0–2 with very little time remaining.

Expected: It recognizes that excessive passivity guarantees defeat and appropriately increases its willingness to take calculated risks.

Scenario E: Recovering Launch Point

The CPU is in danger but has enough stage space to disengage.

Expected: It creates separation, selects a safe area, considers Shield, avoids unnecessary damage, and eventually returns to combat.

The decision should respond to opponent pressure.

Scenario F: No immediate danger

The CPU has reached a safe and useful position, and the opponent is not threatening it.

Expected: The CPU is allowed to remain still without forcing meaningless movement.

Scenario G: Opponent respawning

The opponent is temporarily out of play.

Expected: The CPU repositions, recovers, waits, or prepares for the respawn as appropriate. It does not attack an absent opponent.

Scenario H: Unsafe platform

A platform on Asperitas visibly begins fading.

Expected: The CPU recognizes the changing footing, considers safer surfaces, and avoids treating unavailable geometry as a valid destination.

Scenario I: Stage hazard

A warning appears for an incoming volcanic rock.

Expected: The CPU may react according to its difficulty and awareness, using only information visible after the warning.

Scenario J: Defensive opponent

The opponent repeatedly holds Shield.

Expected: The CPU adapts instead of continuously feeding predictable attacks into the Shield.

It should understand the limitations of the game’s defensive mechanics rather than inventing unblockable options.

Scenario K: Overtime

Scores are tied, overtime is nearly over, and one fighter has lower Launch Point.

Expected: The CPU understands the actual tiebreak rules and chooses offense or survival accordingly.

It must also account for the shrinking Void.

Scenario L: Different fighters

Repeat representative scenarios with all four available fighters and several matchups.

Verify that the logic works through capabilities, not fighter IDs.

Scenario M: CPU versus CPU

Two CPUs choose defensive strategies simultaneously.

Expected: They behave naturally, but do not become stuck in an endless artificial waiting loop.

Scenario N: Changing conditions

The CPU begins retreating, but the opponent becomes vulnerable or a hazard makes its planned position unsafe.

Expected: It reassesses intelligently and changes plans without unnecessary oscillation.

