17. Safe implementation process

Do not attempt this as one uncontrolled rewrite.

Use an incremental approach on a dedicated branch.

Phase 1 — Audit and baseline

Map the existing logic, identify overlaps and limitations, run the tests, and record baseline performance.

Determine exactly why Easy is currently too efficient.

Document the planned architecture before major structural changes.

Phase 2 — Intentional waiting and human movement

Introduce deliberate waiting, eliminate unnecessary movement, redesign the liveness safeguard, and establish clear interruptions and exit conditions.

Verify CPUs do not become permanently passive.

Phase 3 — Defensive recovery and positioning

Implement purposeful retreat, stage-aware safe-position selection, Shield-based defense when appropriate, and Launch Point recovery objectives.

Test the complete disengagement-to-re-engagement sequence.

Phase 4 — Advanced strategic awareness

Extend match-level objectives and connect them to existing strategies.

Improve the distinction between winning an exchange and winning the match.

Support meaningful objective changes based on score, clock, Launch Points, threats, and positional advantage.

Phase 5 — Situational adaptation

Improve stage control, opponent-pattern understanding, resource awareness, positioning, and strategic variety.

Avoid duplicated or competing decision systems.

Phase 6 — Difficulty rebalance

Carefully tune Easy, Medium, Hard, and Brutal using the improved intelligence.

Prioritize Easy’s practical accessibility and preserve human-like decision-making across all levels.

Phase 7 — Validation and refinement

Run all relevant tests and benchmarks.

Add scenario-specific tests.

Fix regressions and pathological behavior.

Evaluate whether the CPU behaves purposefully, not just whether it is winning matches.

Phase 8 — Documentation

Update:

* ALVA_SPEC.md
* docs/systems/ai.md
* Relevant gameplay documentation.
* Difficulty descriptions in js/data/difficulty.js.
* English and French localization where applicable.
* Relevant tests and benchmark documentation.
* UPDATES.md if appropriate.

Remove obsolete claims that CPUs must always move or can never deliberately wait.

Keep documentation synchronized with actual behavior.

